libheif and libde265 (LGPL-3.0)
-------------------------------

Image conversion for HEIC/HEIF inputs uses libheif 1.23.2 (LGPL-3.0-or-later),
built with libde265 1.0.15 (LGPL-3.0-or-later) as its HEVC decoder. Both are
used UNMODIFIED, through the `libheif-js` package, and neither is statically
linked into any binary of ours: this product runs from TypeScript source and
loads libheif at runtime from files on disk.

  - Upstream: https://github.com/strukturag/libheif and
    https://github.com/strukturag/libde265
  - Distribution used: https://github.com/catdad-experiments/libheif-js
  - License texts, as shipped: node_modules/libheif-js/LICENSE (LGPL-3.0) and
    node_modules/libheif-js/libheif/LICENSE (LGPL-3.0, GPL-3.0 and MIT)

REPLACING THE LIBRARY. You may replace libheif with any interface-compatible
build, and nothing of ours needs rebuilding to do it:

  - the WebAssembly binary is a single file, node_modules/libheif-js/
    libheif-wasm/libheif.wasm, and the path it is loaded from is overridable at
    runtime with the VISION_LIBHEIF_WASM_PATH environment variable; or
  - replace the node_modules/libheif-js directory with your own build of the
    package.

The bundled variant of that package (libheif-js/wasm-bundle) is deliberately NOT
what this product loads, for exactly this reason: it carries the same binary
base64-encoded inside a 1.9 MB JavaScript file, which nothing can replace
without rebuilding the package.

HEVC PATENTS, AND THE TWO CASES THIS DECISION DOES NOT COVER. libde265 decodes
HEVC/H.265. Patent licensing for HEVC is administered by pools independent of
these projects (Access Advance among others), and no patent license is granted
by the LGPL, by the Apache License or by this NOTICE. Shipping a decoder
here was decided against ONE distribution model, so the decision is only as good
as that model:

  THE CASE IT WAS DECIDED FOR. The operator installs this product on its own
  infrastructure and decodes images that reach its own agent. That is the same
  exposure every phone, browser and operating system already carries, and the
  alternative is not reading the attachment at all.

  CASE 1, NOT COVERED: HANDING PRE-COMPILED BINARIES TO THIRD PARTIES. Building
  this product into an image, bundle, appliance or installer and distributing
  that artifact to someone else is a different act from self-hosting, and it is
  the act the HEVC pools license. Nothing in this NOTICE, in the LGPL or in
  the Apache License authorizes it, and no assessment of ours reaches it.

  CASE 2, NOT COVERED: A CONTRACT THAT FORBIDS PATENT-POOL EXPOSURE. Some
  customer, procurement or public-sector contracts prohibit shipping components
  exposed to a patent pool, with no regard for how they are deployed. Such a
  clause is decided by the contract and not by this product.

In both cases the capability is REMOVABLE, and removing it is the supported
answer rather than a workaround: replace the library as described above with a
build that carries no HEVC decoder, or point VISION_LIBHEIF_WASM_PATH at a file
that is not there. Measured, so that nobody has to guess what that does: the
conversion fails, the attachment is NOT read, and the application log names the
path it could not load. That is the outcome this feature replaced -- before it,
a HEIC went to the provider as it arrived and the provider refused it -- so the
capability is what is lost, not the installation. Everything else keeps working.

An operator who needs HEIC conversion in a jurisdiction where any of this
matters is responsible for its own assessment.
